= Background
This section presents relevant research and frameworks to establish an overview of research on therapist interfaces for rehabilitation through exergames. This chapter includes three main parts: (1) it establishes our research ground in exergames & automation of rehabilitation work to show existing technology use and automation frameworks, (2) it sets entanglement through co-performance as the theoretical lens, and explores the sense of agency in current research in relation to human-AI systems, (3) it reviews existing therapists interfaces for exergame-based rehabilitation.

== Exergames & Automation in Rehabilitation
Exergames, a fusion of "exercise" and "video games", are video games that promote physical activity and cognitive engagement through gamified tasks @ohyou2010. Although not exclusively, they are often classified under a broader category "Serious Games", which refers to digital games designed primarily for purposes beyond entertainments, such as education, training, and health interventions @Maggio2025. The research also differentiate between commercial off-the-shelf systems and custom systems used for rehabilitation purposes @Allegue2022. The meta-analyses show exergames systems specifically designed for rehabilitation tend to be more effective than conventional rehabilitation, whereas re-purposed commercial off-the-shelf systems often yield mixed or neutral results, in part because their interaction mechanics and feedback are not tightly aligned with therapeutic goals @Maier2019. In essence, exergames have shown to support neuroplasticity by enabling high repetition and high intensity task-oriented activities that are challenging enough but not frustrating resulting in increased motivation and engagement @Maier2019 @Allegue2022 @Shah2022. 

Moreover, a wide range of technologies have been explored to implement exergames for rehabilitation. Non-immersive systems based on Xbox Kinect, Nintendo Wii, PlayStation, robot-assisted serious games, or similar motion-sensing consoles are widely studied and have shown to clinically provide effective rehabilitation compared to conventional rehabilitation @Cheok2015 @Santos2025 @Doumas2025. Similarly, XR-based exergames, which are more immersive in nature, have been used for motor and cognitive rehabilitation after stroke and other neurological conditions, with studies reporting improvements in motor functions, spatial attention, and improved engagement compared to conventional exercises @Fordell2016 @Faria2016. Beside providing a gamified exercise and its effectiveness in clinical treatment, exergames can also be viewed as interactive systems that generate performance data (e.g. reaction times, errors, speed) which can be used adapt game logics in real-time and to monitor progress and support clinical-decision making @Ismail2021. Additionally, AI with Machine Learning (ML) approaches that analyze sensors, game data and performance data aids how interfaces, algorithms and workflows could be designed to support personalized rehabilitation alongside providing gamified exercise experience @AttohMensah2025 @TurmoVidal2025. Exergames, thus, have higher potential of accurately providing and utilizing data-centric monitoring during therapy sessions and create adaptable and fun experiences to fit individual needs, allowing patients to participate in interactive and adaptive exercises @Rahin2025. 

Besides in-game data, automation is increasingly transforming rehabilitation work, with many work processes now being carried out either partially or fully by autonomous systems @TurmoVidal2025. These automated data-driven systems support personalized rehabilitation, and reduce therapists' workload while preserving the human aspects and judgement @Yu2026. Exergame platforms with recommendation systems automatically select and plan personalized exercises based on performance histories, biofeedback, and other data points, shifting agency of clinical decision-making from therapist to algorithms @Ismail2021 @Zhao2020. These studies demonstrate that exergames can be implemented and are being explored in varied technologies across different rehabilitation settings, while autonomous systems are enhancing the work process to provide personalized rehabilitation. However, treating autonomous systems with personalization capabilities as purely technical and user experience optimization would be short-sighted without considering patient preferences, motivational factors and meaningful therapeutic goals.    

Although many rehabilitation technologies do not describe human-in-the-loop (HITL) and human-on-the-loop (HOTL) in the same terms, they still embody these implicit design decisions. In HITL, humans are embedded in the control loop and must initiate, approve or adjust system decisions before they are executed or can move forward in the workflow. For example, when a therapist manually selects the exergames and its parameters or when a therapist manually checks and approves the exergames suggested by the recommendation system. In HOTL, the system operates autonomously while the human monitors and intervenes when required. For example, when an exergame system continuously adjusts game parameters based on patient performance or biofeedback while the therapist supervises through their own interface @Lazaros2026. Various definitions of Level of Automation (LoA) have been used to present how functions are divided between human and AI @AitMalek2020. A recent LoA framework, the General Automation Level Allocation (GALA), presents how functions are divided between human and AI, and emphasize that humans are increasingly supervising semi-autonomous systems rather than directly manipulating every action @Ruscio2024. These frameworks are tools to understand how functions are allocated, how much is automated in any process, and the implications and challenges within each level of collaboration. However, these frameworks do not address how therapists experience agency in relation to automated systems or how it is distributed. 

== Agency, Sense of Agency, and Co-performance
From entanglement or "fourth wave" perspective in HCI, computational systems are not neutral tools used by humans, but co-creators of actions, phenomena, and experiences @Frauenberger2019. This perspective moves away from both human-centered view of human as independent decision-maker operating neutral AI tools, and from the view of AI as autonomous tools that replace humans. Instead, the fourth wave views them as co-performers where humans and computational systems continuously shape each other and perform social practices together @Frauenberger2019 @Kuijer2018. Central to this view is a relational account of agency. Agency is not a property possessed by any individual actor, human or system, rather it emerges through intra-actions between them @Barad2007. According to #cite(<Barad2007>, form: "prose"), intra-actions refers to configuration of entities through their relation, which in turn produces their boundaries, roles, and properties. Therefore, the division between what the human does and what the system does is not predefined or set in advance but enacted. This enacted boundary, termed as agential cut, is the cut that separates and define human and system contributions to a shared practice @Barad2007. Agency in rehabilitation, can be assumed as distributed across humans (therapists and patients) and non-humans (e.g. exergame systems, AI, algorithms, interfaces, sensors, data, institutions, regulations) rather than being possessed by any single actor or entity @GiaccardiRedstrom2020 @Frauenberger2019.

As healthcare systems become more data-driven, decisions are increasingly influenced by algorithmic logic and historical data apart from just clinicians' reasoning  @AttohMensah2025. This means, in the context of exergame-based rehabilitation, rehabilitation outcomes would be shaped not only by therapists' decisions and patients' performance, but also by how algorithms classify performance, how data is presented, what data is shown or hidden, what technology is used, and even institutional and legal constraints. This reiterates the fact that design in therapeutic scenarios is more about configuring relationships and agencies among multiple human and non-human actors over time, rather than just optimizing features within an interface. The entanglement ontology that grounds this view is generative but broad. The wider more-than-human literature it belongs to also encompasses ecological and multispecies concerns that fall outside the scope of clinical rehabilitation settings. Co-performance narrows this entanglement ontology and grounds it in practice, in which AI systems are understood as co-performers that practice alongside humans, their roles and responsibilities are renegotiated as the practice unfolds @Kuijer2018.

Differing from agency, the sense of agency is the subjective experience of control over one's action and its outcome @Limerick2014. Thus, rather than concerning with agency configuration across actors during a shared practice, the sense of agency is concerned with the individual feeling of being in control @Limerick2014. This sense of agency can be negatively affected by higher cognitive workload, and higher level automation @Zanatto2021 @Limerick2014. #cite(<Legaspi2024>, form: "prose") extends these ideas to human-AI systems and argue they should be designed to preserve this sense of control and agency. It is also emphasized that clinicians should still be able to intervene when co-performing with AI, because legal and ethical responsibility and accountability for decisions currently remains with them @Olawade2026. Additionally, there is a positive correlation between frequent reliance on AI tools and reduced clinical reasoning or decision-making skills and actions @Gerlich2025. The human agency is, thus, not replaced by AI because it is central to the process and continuously negotiated with the system @Frauenberger2019. However, delegating more decision-making to the autonomous systems can create power imbalances. For this reason, a more balanced approach where systems "performs" but the judgement of what is good, bad, or appropriate ultimately remains with the human, is recommended @Kuijer2018. 

This concern is more pronounced for novice therapists, who rely more on informative but objective factors for clinical decision-making compared to experienced therapists, who consider both subjective and objective data to provide personalized rehabilitation @Holdar2013. Subjective informations like patient preferences and feedback, patient motivation, physical and psychological state, and therapists' own work and life experience were among the factors that played crucial role, as well as prioritized, in clinical reasoning of physiotherapists besides just objective data. This clinical reasoning helps them set patient goals, decide treatment plans, and adjust rehabilitation sessions @Molle2025 @Holdar2013. This highlights the need to either incorporate or consider subjective data when developing technology for rehabilitation process, suggesting therapists should be in the loop instead of adopting full automation @Molle2025. Studies on Therapeutic Alliance (TA), support this idea as maintaining a collaborative relationship between a clinician and their patient is a necessity for rehabilitation helping them shape adherence, motivation, perception of quality of care and ultimately rehabilitation outcomes. @Kayes2012.  

== Therapist Interfaces for Exergame-Based Rehabilitation <therapistinterfaces>
Various therapist interfaces for personalized rehabilitation through exergames have been developed, although they do not account for the entirety of the rehabilitation process as they mainly focus on data documentation and/or session planning and task manipulation. One therapist interface for Village Druid VR, a therapy game used for rehabilitation was prototyped through 2 co-design sessions with 2 therapists each. The lo-fi and hi-fi prototypes focused on patient data documentation for monitoring and progress evaluation as well as some simpler personalization session parameters which were not clearly defined @Ferreira2022. Similarly, #cite(<Duval2022>, form: "prose") redesigned an already existing conventional VR game, Spellcasters, through co-design sessions with 14 medical professionals (PT's & OT's). The re-purposed version of Spellcasters focused again solely on data documentation deemed valuable by the medical professionals, and task management through custom rehabilitative gestures to adjust relevant variables including scale, shape, direction and depth.

Another more elaborative study developed a set of collaborative VR serious games, 4 in total, both for upper-limb physical rehabilitation and cognitive rehabilitation of stroke survivors. Again, their framework ADAPT (Adaptive Dynamics for Activity-based Personalized Therapy) only focused on personalized session planning and some game task parameters. For the session planning, therapists could adjust the number of participants, the selection and sequencing of games, session durations and individualized goals. For game task parameters, the therapist could adjust level of difficulty and more detailed adjustments in complexity such as the number and type of objects or puzzle pieces, and in-game payment processes @Figueiredo2025-ADAPT. One study in particular fully isolated game task adjustment in the form of difficulty parameters only. Difficulty in classification of levels were found to lead to longer playtime with more fun and higher performance when compared to high-commitment game parameter goals in terms of goal and time @Eckert2023. #cite(<GagnonRoy2025>, form: "prose"), on the other hand co-developed COOK, a cognitive assistance system together with occupational therapists to help patients regain the ability to prepare meals. Although not an exergame, the patient still has a personalized interface with step-by-step instructions to follow including breaks, reminders and recipe-steps which are all pre-configured by the therapist. 

With one exception, previous research does not account for how to share the agency between the therapist and the system. #cite(<Figueiredo2025HybridControl>, form: "prose"), the exception, highlight in their study that 7 out of 10 participants preferred a hybrid mode combining both automated and manual adjustments, however focused on task personalization only as well. Furthermore, while data documentation does improve evaluation, decision-making and personalized rehabilitation, ultimately improving patient care @Tadas2023 @Dowding2015, the current systems still remain very fragmented as they lack a holistic view of all the main phases in the rehabilitation process. However, existing literature does provide an overview of the main phases of the rehabilitations process within occupational therapy and physiotherapy, yet it is still not accounted for in rehabilitation systems. In the "_Occupational Therapy Intervention Process model_" the core phases are: (1) Evaluation and Goal-Setting Phase, (2) Intervention Phase, (3) Reevaluation Phase @Hinojosa_Kramer_Royeen_2017 @fisher2009occupational. Similarly, physiotherapy, often referred to as the "rehab cycle" also includes (1) Assessment and goal setting, (2) intervention and (3) re-evaluation @Negrini2019 @Physiopedia.

In summary, the existing rehabilitation systems are fragmented across different platforms for assessment, rehabilitation, and documentation. Furthermore, the current therapist interfaces for exergame rehabilitation systems primarily focus on adjustable parameters, dashboards, automated adjustments and effective recommendations, rather than on how control and responsibility are shared between therapists and AI-supported systems across the main phases of the rehabilitation process. 


